course: world
lesson: 11
title: Solids, liquids and gases
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Everything around us is made of matter: anything that has mass and takes up space, even the air.
explain: Matter is made of tiny particles, far too small to see, and they are always moving.
explain: In a solid the particles are packed tightly and only jiggle in place, so a solid keeps its own shape.
explain: In a liquid the particles are close together but slide past each other, so a liquid flows and takes the shape of its container, but keeps its volume.
explain: In a gas the particles are far apart and fly about fast, so a gas spreads out to fill any container it is in.
explain: For example, the same water can be a hard ice cube, a liquid in a glass, or invisible water vapour in the air.
fact: A solid has a fixed shape and a fixed volume.
fact: A liquid has a fixed volume but takes the shape of its container.
fact: A gas has no fixed shape or volume; it spreads out to fill its container.
fact: Gases can be squashed into a smaller space much more easily than solids or liquids.
fact: Air is a gas; it has mass and takes up space even though we cannot see it.
fact: Water vapour is water as a gas, and it is invisible.
fact: A smell is made of tiny particles of gas that spread out through the air until some reach your nose.
fact: Sand and sugar can be poured, but each grain is a tiny solid with its own shape.
fact: The particles of a gas are much farther apart than those of a solid or a liquid.
fact: Heating makes particles move faster; cooling makes them move more slowly.
exercise: Which of these is a liquid? | Milk | A wooden spoon | The air in a balloon | A pebble
exercise: What happens when you pour juice from a tall glass into a wide bowl? | It takes the shape of the bowl and keeps its volume | It keeps the shape of the tall glass | It spreads out to fill the whole bowl right up to the top | It turns into a gas in the bowl
exercise: Which state of matter spreads out to fill its whole container? | A gas | A solid | A liquid | None of them
exercise: Why does a stone keep its shape? | Its particles are packed tightly and only jiggle in place | Its particles are not moving at all | It has no particles inside it | Its particles are far apart from each other but cannot move
exercise: Is air matter? | Yes, because it has mass and takes up space | No, because we cannot see it | No, because it weighs nothing at all | Only when the wind is blowing
exercise: Sand can be poured like water. What state is sand? | A solid made of many tiny grains | A liquid, because it pours | A gas, because it spreads out | Half a liquid and half a gas
exercise: Which is easiest to squash into a smaller space? | Air in a closed syringe | Water in a closed syringe | A block of wood | A steel ball
exercise: In which state are the particles farthest apart? | Gas | Solid | Liquid | The same in all three
exercise: What is water vapour? | Water as an invisible gas | Tiny drops of liquid water | Very small pieces of ice | White smoke from hot water
exercise: What happens to particles when matter is heated? | They move faster | They grow bigger | They stop moving | They disappear
exercise: Which of these has a fixed volume but no fixed shape? | Orange juice | A brick | A pencil | Helium in a balloon
exercise: Why can you smell a cake baking from another room? | Gas particles from the cake spread through the air | Tiny solid crumbs float across the room | The smell travels only along the floor | Your nose makes the smell up by itself when you are hungry
exercise: What is the same about ice, liquid water and water vapour? | They are all made of the same water particles | They all have a fixed shape | They can all flow | They are all invisible
exercise: Honey is thick and flows slowly. Which state is it? | A liquid, because it flows and fits its jar | A solid, because it is so thick | A gas, because it is so sticky | A special state that is none of them
test: You blow up a balloon. What is inside it that makes it fat? | Air, a gas that takes up space | Nothing at all, it is empty | Only the warmth from your body | Only water from your breath
test: A full bottle of water is poured into an empty bottle of another shape. Which is true? | The amount of water stays the same | There is less water because the shape changed | There is more water in the wider bottle | Part of the water has turned to gas
test: Why can a gas be squeezed but a solid hardly at all? | Gas particles have lots of space between them | Gas particles are softer than solid ones | Solid particles are much bigger than gas ones | Gases have no particles in them
test: Which list has one solid, one liquid and one gas? | Chalk, vinegar, oxygen | Chalk, sugar, oxygen | Vinegar, oil, oxygen | Chalk, ice, olive oil
test: Someone opens a bottle of perfume in a corner, and soon the whole room smells of it. Why? | The perfume gas spreads out to fill the room | The perfume stays near the bottle | The perfume turns into a solid powder | The room gets warmer from the perfume
test: Which is true about a block of ice? | Its particles keep jiggling even though it is solid | Its particles are completely still | It has no particles until it melts | Its particles are much farther apart than they are in a gas
test: A jelly wobbles but keeps its shape on a plate. Which state is it closest to? | A solid | A liquid | A gas | It changes state every time it wobbles
test: What happens to the particles of hot soup as it cools? | They move more slowly | They shrink to half their size | They stop moving all at once | They become a different substance
test: Which can fill a whole empty room on its own? | A gas | A liquid | A solid | Any of them, if you wait
test: why's the juice go flat in the glass n not stay in a carton shape | Liquids take the shape of their container | Juice is a solid with a soft shape | The glass squeezes it into a new shape | Liquids have no volume of their own
test: can u squash air in a syringe if u block the end | Yes, gases can be squashed into a smaller space | No, air can't be squashed at all, it just pushes back | Only if the air is warm | No, only water can be squashed
test: Tom says air weighs nothing cos you can't see it. true? | No, air has mass and takes up space | Yes, invisible things have no mass | Yes, air only weighs something when it's wet | No, but it only takes up space when it's moving
test: Leo reckons sand's a liquid cos you can pour it. right? | No, each grain is a tiny solid with its own shape | Yes, anything you can pour must be a liquid, like water | Yes, sand is a liquid that is very dry | No, sand is a gas because it blows away
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a liquid? | It possesses a fixed volume but assumes the shape of its container | It possesses a fixed shape and a fixed volume | It possesses neither a fixed shape nor a fixed volume, but expands freely | It possesses a fixed shape but no fixed volume
exam: Identify, from among the following, the state of matter most readily compressed. | A gas | A liquid | A solid | All states are compressed equally
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning water vapour. | It is water in the gaseous state and is invisible | It is the white cloud issuing from a kettle | It is a solid composed of small crystals | It is a liquid of reduced density
exam: Determine the effect of heating upon the constituent particles of a substance. | They move more rapidly | They move more slowly | They become larger in size | They cease to move entirely
